The '''2018 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League]], the 63rd season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 26th season since it was renamed from the European Cup to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex|NSC Olimpiyskiy]] in [[Kyiv]], Ukraine on 26 May 2018,<ref name="host">{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0cd2199fd758-fda0db2ab4b4-1000--kyiv-to-host-2018-champions-league-final/ |title=Kyiv to host 2018 Champions League final |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=15 September 2016 |access-date=15 September 2016}}</ref> between [[Royal Spanish Football Federation|Spanish]] side and defending champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], who had won the competition in each of the previous two seasons, and [[The Football Association|English]] side [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], making their eighth final appearance and first since [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98c00a9589-71ace0d77127-1000--all-you-need-to-know-about-the-champions-league-final/|title=All you need to know about the Champions League final|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98c00a7409-62dd9337a041-1000--madrid-v-liverpool-meet-the-champions-league-finalists/|title=Madrid v Liverpool: meet the Champions League finalists|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018}}</ref> The two sides had previously met in the [[1981 European Cup final|1981 final]].

After Liverpool's [[Sadio Mané]] cancelled out [[Karim Benzema]]'s opener for Real Madrid, two goals from man of the match [[Gareth Bale]] proved the difference in a 3–1 win for the Spaniards, making them the first team to win three back-to-back titles in the Champions League era and the first since [[1975–76 FC Bayern Munich season|Bayern Munich]] defeated [[AS Saint-Étienne|Saint-Étienne]] in the [[1976 European Cup final]]; it was additionally their fourth title in five seasons and their 13th European Cup overall. They also earned the right to play the winners of the [[2017–18 UEFA Europa League]], [[Atlético Madrid]], in the [[2018 UEFA Super Cup]] and to enter the semi-finals of the [[2018 FIFA Club World Cup]], losing the former and winning the latter. Additionally, Real Madrid qualified to enter the group stage of the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d81c2c9caf3-d5f66f92b4db-1000--evolution-of-uefa-club-competitions-from-2018/|title=Evolution of UEFA club competitions from 2018|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=26 August 2016}}</ref> but since they already qualified through their league performance, the berth reserved was given to the champions of the [[2017–18 Czech First League]], the 11th-ranked association according to next season's access list.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0248-0e98d6a7579b-b83f689f894f-1000--who-is-in-the-champions-league-group-stage/|title=Who is in the 2018/19 Champions League group stage?|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=26 May 2018}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Olimpiysky Stadium, Kiev - panoramio.jpg|thumb|The [[Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex|NSC Olimpiyskiy]] in [[Kyiv]] hosted the final]]
The [[Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex|NSC Olimpiyskiy]] was announced as the final venue on 15 September 2016, following the decision of the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Athens]], [[Greece]].<ref name="host"/> This was the sixth European Cup/Champions League final hosted at an [[Eastern Europe]]an venue following those in [[1973 European Cup final|1973]] in [[Belgrade]], [[Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia|Yugoslavia]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1972/overview/index.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130412013255/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1972/overview/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 April 2013 |title=1972–73 season |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018 |access-date=2 May 2018}}</ref> the [[1983 European Cup final|1983]], [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]] finals hosted by Athens, Greece,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1983/overview/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111207010325/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1983/overview/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=7 December 2011 |title=1982–83 season |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018 |access-date=2 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1994/overview/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111203044124/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=1994/overview/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 December 2011 |title=1993–94 season |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018 |access-date=2 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Michael |last=Harrold |title=Inzaghi inspires Milan to glory |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01c1-0e6e500c2c66-db2412e7ba39-1000--inzaghi-inspires-milan-to-glory/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 May 2007 |access-date=2 May 2018 }}</ref> as well as the [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008 final]] in [[Moscow]], [[Russia]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/season=2008/?iv=true |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180503112446/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/season=2008/?iv=true |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 May 2018 |title=2007–08 season |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018 |access-date=2 May 2018}}</ref>

The Olimpiyskiy Stadium was built in 1923 and it has been renovated three times, most recently in 2011 in preparation for the [[UEFA Euro 2012]] tournament.<ref>{{cite news|title=Kyiv opens host stadium for Euro 2012 final|url=https://www.kyivpost.com/news/city/detail/114397/|work=[[Kyiv Post]]|date=9 October 2011 |access-date=2 May 2018}}</ref> The stadium was used as a venue in the [[1980 Summer Olympics]] for its [[Football at the 1980 Summer Olympics|football]] event and the aforementioned European Championship,<ref>{{cite web|title=FIFA Technical Report – 1980 Olympics Football Tournament|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/afdeveloping/technicaldevp/50/05/10/oft_80_tr_167.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111220063559/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/afdeveloping/technicaldevp/50/05/10/oft%5f80%5ftr%5f167.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=20 December 2011|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|access-date=2 May 2018|date=1980}}</ref> including the [[UEFA Euro 2012 Final|final match]], which saw [[Spain national football team|Spain]] beat [[Italian national football team|Italy]] by the record-breaking score of 4–0 on the way to their third title.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/jul/01/euro-2012-spain-italy-final |title= Spain 4 Italy 0 Match Report |date= 1 July 2012 |work=Guardian UK |access-date=2 May 2018 }}</ref> Its current capacity is 70,050 and it is used by the [[Ukraine national football team]], [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dynamo Kyiv]], and major domestic matches like the [[Ukrainian Cup]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Facts & Figures |publisher=NSC Olimpiyskiy Stadium |url=http://nsc-olimpiyskiy.com.ua/en/stadium/facts |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120712221406/http://nsc-olimpiyskiy.com.ua/en/stadium/facts |archive-date=12 July 2012 |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=2018 UEFA Champions League final: Guide to Kyiv |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2018/final/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170623201856/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2018/final/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=23 June 2017 |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref>

==Background==
Defending champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] reached a record 16th final after a 4–3 aggregate win against [[Bundesliga|German]] side [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], knocking them out of the competition for the second consecutive season. This was Real Madrid's third consecutive final, and fourth final in five tournaments with an opportunity to win a [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics|record 13th title]]. Previously they won finals in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1958 European Cup final|1958]], [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]], [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]], [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]] and [[2017 UEFA Champions League final|2017]]; and lost in [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] and [[1981 European Cup final|1981]]. This was also their 20th final in all seasonal UEFA competitions, having also played in two [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (losing in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1971]] and [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1983]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1985 UEFA Cup Final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup Final|1986]]). Real Madrid are only the third team since the competition's rebranding as the Champions League to reach three consecutive finals after [[AC Milan|Milan]] in [[1995 UEFA Champions League final|1995]] and [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] in [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]]. They were the first team in the Champions League era, and the fourth overall, to win three straight finals, a feat only achieved by the Real Madrid side of the 1950s, as they went on to win a record five successive finals, as well as the [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] and [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] squads of the 1970s in [[1973 European Cup final|1973]] and [[1976 European Cup final|1976]], respectively.<ref>{{cite web|last=Saffer|first=Paul|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e9cd8be7edb-c0384dc7e939-1000--three-in-a-row-real-madrid-make-more-history/|title=Three in a row: Real Madrid making final history|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 May 2018}}</ref>

[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] reached their eighth final, their first since [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]], after a 7–6 aggregate win against Italian side [[AS Roma|Roma]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Grez |first=Matias |date=2 May 2018 |title=Liverpool see off spirited Roma to reach Champions League final |url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/05/02/football/roma-liverpool-champions-league-mo-salah/index.html |publisher=[[CNN]] |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> They had won the final on five occasions ([[1977 European Cup final|1977]], [[1978 European Cup final|1978]], [[1981 European Cup final|1981]], [[1984 European Cup final|1984]] and [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]]), and lost twice ([[1985 European Cup final|1985]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]]). This was also their 13th final in UEFA seasonal competitions, having played in one [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] final (losing in [[1966 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1966]]) and four [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup/Europa League]] finals (winning in [[1973 UEFA Cup Final|1973]], [[1976 UEFA Cup Final|1976]] and [[2001 UEFA Cup Final|2001]]; and losing in [[2016 UEFA Europa League Final|2016]]).<ref>{{cite web |date=2 June 2017 |title=Club facts: Liverpool |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0e75573bf39d-f79018a82bc9-1000--liverpool-facts/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> Liverpool were the first team since [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in [[2011–12 UEFA Champions League|2011–12]] to reach the final having qualified for the competition through the [[2017-18 UEFA Champions League qualifying phase and play-off round|play-off round]]. This was also the most recent occasion the final featured an English team ([[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]).<ref>{{cite news |last=Johnston |first=Neil |date=2 May 2018 |title=Roma 4-2 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/43900126 |website=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> Liverpool were the most recent team to defeat Real Madrid in a European Cup final, winning 1–0 in Paris in [[1981 European Cup final|1981]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Corrigan |first=Dermot |date=25 May 2018 |title=Champions League final repeat of 1981 shows tables have turned at Madrid, Liverpool |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/uefa-champions-league/775/blog/post/3509045/champions-league-final-repeat-of-1981-shows-tables-have-turned-at-madrid-liverpool |website=ESPN.co.uk |publisher=ESPN Internet Ventures |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref>

Besides the 1981 final, the two teams had played each other four times in the Champions League era. Liverpool won both matches in the [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League]] round of 16 (1–0 away and 4–0 at home), while Real Madrid won both matches in the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]] group stage (3–0 away and 1–0 at home).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98c1443e9f-c00db0c22ccf-1000--real-madrid-v-liverpool-detailed-head-to-head/|title=Real Madrid v Liverpool: detailed head-to-head|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=3 May 2018}}</ref>

