==Pre-match==
===Ambassador===
[[File:Andriy Shevchenko 2015.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Andriy Shevchenko]] was named as the ambassador for the final.]]

The ambassador for the final was former Ukrainian international [[Andriy Shevchenko]], who won the UEFA Champions League with Milan in [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]].<ref>{{cite news|title=All you need to know about the Champions League final|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98c00a9589-71ace0d77127-1000--all-you-need-to-know-about-the-champions-league-final/|access-date=3 May 2018|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 May 2018}}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 63,000 for the final, a total of 40,700 tickets were available to fans and the general public, with the two finalist teams receiving 17,000 tickets each and with 6,700 tickets being available for sale to fans worldwide via UEFA.com from 15 to 22 March 2018 in four price categories: €450, €320, €160, and €70. The remaining tickets were allocated to the local organising committee, UEFA and national associations, commercial partners and broadcasters, and to serve the corporate hospitality programme.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0243-0e988951368b-63edbf80a9f2-1000--champions-league-final-ticket-window-closed/ |title=UEFA Champions League final ticket application window |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=12 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/newsid=2544816.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180316112117/http://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/newsid=2544816.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=16 March 2018|title=2018 UEFA Champions League final ticket sales launched|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 March 2018}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
[[File:Dua Lipa 'One Kiss' UEFA.png|thumb|left|Dua Lipa performing "[[One Kiss (song)|One Kiss]]" at the opening ceremony.]]

English singer [[Dua Lipa]] performed at the opening ceremony preceding the final, by singing multiple songs (made by her) including her famous "One Kiss" and the fan-favorite "No Lie".<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Dua Lipa to Perform at UEFA Champions League Opening Ceremony: 'There'll Be a Whole Lot of Girl Power'|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/pop/dua-lipa-performing-uefa-champions-league-opening-ceremony-soccer-8455039/|magazine=Billboard|date=13 May 2018}}</ref> Jamaican rapper [[Sean Paul]] joined her as a special guest to perform their collaborative song, "[[No Lie (Sean Paul song)|No Lie]]".<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.loopjamaica.com/content/sean-paul-returns-stage-after-fathers-death|title=Sean Paul returns to stage after father's death|website=www.loopjamaica.com|language=en|access-date=29 May 2018|archive-date=19 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519032405/http://www.loopjamaica.com/content/sean-paul-returns-stage-after-fathers-death|url-status=dead}}</ref> The [[UEFA Champions League Anthem]] was performed by Croatian [[cello]] duo [[2Cellos]].<ref>{{cite news |title=2Cellos to perform UEFA Champions League anthem in Kyiv |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0245-0f8e60897b00-eadbfb0bfc6c-1000--2cellos-to-perform-uefa-champions-league-anthem-in-kyiv/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 May 2018 |access-date=23 June 2019 }}</ref>

===Related events===
The [[2018 UEFA Women's Champions League final]] was held two days earlier, on 24 May 2018, at the [[Valeriy Lobanovskyi Dynamo Stadium]] between [[VfL Wolfsburg (women)|Wolfsburg]] and [[Olympique Lyonnais Féminin|Lyon]], Lyon emerging victorious 4–1.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/may/24/wolfsburg-lyon-womens-champions-league-final-match-report|title=Lyon sweep to Women's Champions League win over 10-player Wolfsburg|work=The Guardian|last=Wrack|first=Suzanne|date=24 May 2018}}</ref> This was also the last time that the host city for the men's Champions League final was also automatically assigned the Women's Champions League final.<ref>{{cite news |date=24 May 2018 |title=Lyon beats Wolfsburg 4-1 to win Women's Champions League |url=http://www.stltoday.com/sports/lyon-beats-wolfsburg---to-win-women-s-champions/article_8c91c685-4598-519e-a84b-24388ae7a1ef.html |work=[[St. Louis Post-Dispatch]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=26 May 2018 }}{{Dead link|date=December 2021 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

The annual UEFA Champions Festival was held between 24 and 27 May 2018 in the Kyiv city centre.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98ce63fb97-8d24bff95c67-1000--uefa-champions-festival-in-kyiv-during-final-week/|title=UEFA Champions Festival in Kyiv during final week|website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 May 2018}}</ref>

===Incidents===
In late May, ''[[The New York Times]]'' reported that some fans with allocated tickets had returned them after having trouble finding flights to and accommodation in Kyiv. Locals in Kyiv began offering free accommodation for fans affected by cancelled hotel and apartment arrangements.<ref>{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Rory |date=20 May 2018 |title=Why Was My Room Canceled? A Final Overwhelms Kiev. |page=D1 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/21/sports/soccer/champions-league-liverpool-real-madrid.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=22 May 2018}}</ref> Several [[charter flights]] arranged for Liverpool fans were cancelled, leaving fans with tickets stranded and leading to an unsuccessful search for alternative solutions by the club and city government.<ref>{{cite news |date=26 May 2018 |title=Champions League final: Fans gather in Kiev after flight cancellations |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-merseyside-44258337 |website=BBC News|access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=26 May 2018 |title=Liverpool mayor 'gutted' after Kiev flights cancelled |url=https://news.sky.com/story/liverpool-mayor-joe-anderson-speaks-to-kievs-vitali-klitschko-after-fans-flights-cancelled-11385466 |publisher=[[Sky News]] |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> Other airlines offered flights to Kyiv from airports in Liverpool and Manchester, using assigned slots at Kyiv's airports.<ref>{{cite news |last=Calder |first=Simon |date=25 May 2018 |title=Champions League final 2018: What went wrong with cancelled flights to Kiev? |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/travel/news-and-advice/champions-league-final-2018-flights-cancelled-real-madrid-vs-liverpool-plane-kiev-uefa-a8369711.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/travel/news-and-advice/champions-league-final-2018-flights-cancelled-real-madrid-vs-liverpool-plane-kiev-uefa-a8369711.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=The Independent |access-date=26 May 2018}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

On 24 May, a group of Liverpool fans were attacked in a restaurant by 20 masked hooligans.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/world-news/breaking-masked-hooligans-attack-liverpool-12592718|title=Masked hooligans attack Liverpool fans like a 'pack of dogs' in horrifying ambush ahead of Champions League final in Kiev|website=Mirror.co.uk|date=24 May 2018}}</ref>

==Match==
===Officials===
On 7 May 2018, UEFA announced that Serbian [[Milorad Mažić]] would officiate the final. Mažić has been a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] since 2009, and gained UEFA's elite referee status in 2013. He was joined by his fellow countrymen, with Milovan Ristić and Dalibor Đurđević as assistant referees, Nenad Đokić and Danilo Grujić as additional assistant referees, and Nemanja Petrović as reserve assistant referee. The fourth official for the final was Frenchman [[Clément Turpin]].<ref name="officials">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e9cd49b19c6-df2dfa498bfa-1000--milorad-mazic-to-referee-champions-league-final/ |title=Milorad Mažić to referee Champions League final |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=7 May 2018 |access-date=7 May 2018}}</ref>

===Summary===
[[File:Gareth Bale 2015 (9).jpg|thumb|right|[[Gareth Bale]] entered the match as a substitute in the 61st minute and scored two goals for Madrid.|193x193px]]

The match began with Liverpool's kickoff and the team's successive attacks to counter Madrid's slower, possession-based buildup. In the 23rd minute, a low shot by [[Trent Alexander-Arnold]] went through a defender's legs and forced a late save by [[Keylor Navas]]. Two minutes later, Liverpool forward [[Mohamed Salah]] was injured competing for the ball with [[Sergio Ramos]], who had [[Chicken wing tackle|locked Salah's arm]] resulting in a fall.<ref name="Guardian">{{cite news |last=Taylor |first=Daniel |date=26 May 2018 |title=Real Madrid win Champions League as brilliant Bale sinks Liverpool |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/may/26/real-madrid-liverpool-champions-league-final-match-report |work=The Guardian |access-date=26 May 2018 |quote=Ramos had locked Salah's right arm and turned him, judo-style, as they lost balance going for the same ball. }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=26 May 2018 |title=Salah and Karius mark dark night for Reds |url=https://theworldgame.sbs.com.au/salah-and-karius-mark-dark-night-for-reds |publisher=[[Special Broadcasting Service|SBS]] |access-date=26 May 2018 |quote=They jeered Madrid captain Ramos, who appeared to have had Salah in an armlock as they fell to the ground }}</ref> Due to a [[dislocated shoulder]], Salah was substituted four minutes later for [[Adam Lallana]].<ref name="Guardian-Live">{{cite news |last=Smyth |first=Rob |date=26 May 2018 |title=Real Madrid v Liverpool: Champions League final 2018 – live! |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2018/may/26/real-madrid-v-liverpool-champions-league-final-2018-live |work=The Guardian |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> Madrid's [[Dani Carvajal]] was substituted in the 37th minute with a [[hamstring]] injury after an unsuccessful [[backheel]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Price |first=Glenn |date=26 May 2018 |title=Liverpool's Mohamed Salah, Real Madrid's Dani Carvajal injured in Champions League final |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/soccer/liverpool/story/3510388/liverpools-mohamed-salah-and-real-madrids-dani-carvajal-injured-in-champions-league-final |work=ESPN.co.uk |publisher=ESPN Internet Ventures|access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> Minutes later, [[Karim Benzema]] appeared to score by finishing a shot started by a [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] header. His goal was disallowed however because he was judged to have been in an offside position. The first half ended scoreless, with Madrid dominating possession but Liverpool having more chances to score.<ref name="Guardian"/><ref name="Guardian-Live"/>
[[File:Loris Karius.jpg|thumb|260x260px|[[Loris Karius]] during the game.]]
The first chance of the second half fell to [[Isco]], who hit the crossbar. In the 51st minute, Benzema scored the match's first goal by deflecting a throw by Liverpool goalkeeper [[Loris Karius]] back into the net. Liverpool equalised four minutes later with a tap-in by [[Sadio Mané]], who finished a header by [[Dejan Lovren]] after a corner kick by [[James Milner]] from the right.<ref name="BBC">{{cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=26 May 2018 |title=Real Madrid 3-1 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44258022 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> [[Gareth Bale]] was substituted in for Isco in the 61st minute and scored Madrid's second goal two minutes later, using an acrobatic [[bicycle kick]] to finish a cross by [[Marcelo (footballer, born 1988)|Marcelo]] from the left.<ref name="NYT">{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Rory |last2=Das |first2=Andrew |date=26 May 2018 |title=Real Madrid Beats Liverpool in Champions League final on a Wonder and Two Blunders |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/26/sports/champions-league-real-madrid-liverpool.html |work=The New York Times |access-date=26 May 2018}}</ref> Liverpool pressed for a second equalising goal, with a shot by Mané that hit the goal post and calls for a [[penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] for an alleged handball, but were also losing possession to Madrid. Ronaldo had a chance to score his first goal of the match during a counter-attack in the 73rd minute, but was tackled by Liverpool defender [[Andy Robertson]] in the penalty box. Bale scored his second goal of the match in the 83rd minute on a {{convert|40|yd|adj=mid}} shot that swerved in front of Karius and went through his hands and into the net. A second chance on goal for Ronaldo in the 93rd minute of play was interrupted by a [[pitch invasion|pitch invader]], who was captured by stadium stewards.<ref name="Guardian" /><ref name="Guardian-Live" />

